# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Tool write-ups (description field) for Symmetry. Keyed by tool media id."""
DESCRIPTIONS = {
"M1.S2.T2.C4.2D1": (
"A sheet of paper lies on the screen with one figure printed on it, and a long dashed fold line lies across the figure. "
"The student can drag the fold line to any place and turn it to a vertical, horizontal or slanted direction. Beside the sheet are a Fold button, "
"an overlap meter that reads from 0 to 100 percent, and a list headed Lines found.\n\n"
"The student first says Yes or No to the question: will this fold work? Then the student presses Fold. One side of the figure flips over the line while a faint ghost stays where the other side was. "
"If the halves overlap exactly, the meter reads 100 percent, the line turns green and is added to the list. If a corner sticks out, the part that does not match is shown in red and the meter stays below 100, "
"so a figure that only looks balanced is seen not to fold onto itself. On a rectangle that is not a square, the vertical and horizontal folds work, but the fold along a diagonal leaves two red corners. "
"Pressing Fold again opens the sheet for the next try, and the student can try as many lines as needed.\n\n"
"The six figures are an isosceles triangle (1 line), a square (4 lines: vertical, horizontal and both diagonals), a rectangle that is not a square (2 lines, and the diagonal fails), "
"a kite that is not a rhombus (1 line), a scalene triangle (no line) and a regular hexagon (6 lines). After trying folds, the student keeps the lines that overlap and enters how many there are, and 0 is allowed for none. "
"A wrong count shows the lines found so far on the figure and asks the student to try another direction. A solved card shows every line of symmetry as a dashed line with the two halves overlapping.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: what does it mean for two halves to overlap exactly? Then: try a vertical, a horizontal and a slanted fold on the figure. Then: press Fold on a line and look for any part that sticks out. "
"Last, the lines that overlap are shown as dashed lines, and a similar figure follows. Through doing, the student learns that a line is a line of symmetry only when folding along it makes the two parts overlap exactly, "
"and that a figure can have one line, several lines or none."
),
"M1.S6.T6.C20.2D1": (
"A square grid fills the screen. One bold blue mirror line runs across it, and half of a figure is drawn on one side, with its corners marked as dots. A Reflect button sits below the grid, "
"and the later cards show a second blue line.\n\n"
"For each corner dot the student first predicts where its mirror partner will land, then taps or drags to that grid point. A dashed link at once joins the dot to the partner and crosses the blue line at right angles, "
"with equal tick marks drawn on the two sides to show the distances to the line. If the partner is on the wrong side, or at a different distance, the link shows the unequal distances and the student moves the partner. "
"A dot that already sits on the line cannot go anywhere: it is shown staying fixed, with its partner on top of itself. Pressing Reflect checks the partners placed, or drops the missing ones one by one if the student is stuck. "
"When every partner is down, the student joins the dots in order to finish the figure.\n\n"
"The six cards are: a vertical line with a 3-square L-shape on one side; a horizontal line with a triangle touching it, whose corners on the line stay put; a diagonal line with a one-square staircase, "
"where the dashed link slants and still meets the line at right angles; a vertical line that the figure crosses, so points on both sides are reflected; two lines, vertical and horizontal, "
"with a small shape in one corner, where the student reflects in the first line and then reflects the whole figure in the second to get 4 copies in all; and two diagonal lines with a small shape in one gap, again 4 copies in all. "
"A solved card shows the completed figure with its line, or lines, as dashed lines.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: which side of the line is the figure on now? Then: each point goes to the other side at the same distance from the line. Then: count the squares from the point to the line, and count the same number on the other side. "
"Last, the partner is shown with its dashed link, and a similar figure follows. Through doing, the student learns that every point and its mirror partner are the same distance from the line on opposite sides, "
"that points on the line stay where they are, and that two lines of symmetry are used one after the other."
),
"M1.S8.T8.C28.2D1": (
"A square sheet of paper lies flat in the middle of the screen. Beside it are three fold buttons, vertical, horizontal and diagonal, a fold counter with a layer count that starts at 1, "
"a Punch tool, a Cut tool and an Unfold button.\n\n"
"The student chooses folds and the sheet folds with a short animation. Each fold halves the shape and doubles the layers, so after a vertical fold and then a horizontal fold the layer count reads 4. "
"The student predicts how many holes the opened sheet will have, then taps the folded sheet with Punch, or draws one straight line across its edge with Cut, and presses Unfold. "
"The sheet opens to show the holes, and every fold is drawn as a dashed line. The student can see that each hole has a mirror partner across each fold line, and that a fold line sits halfway between a pair of matching holes, "
"at right angles to the line joining them.\n\n"
"The six cards are: one vertical fold and one punch, how many holes (2); a diagonal fold and one punch (2 holes); a vertical fold then a horizontal fold and one punch (4 holes); "
"two holes on the left and right at the same height, which fold made them (vertical); four holes, one near each corner, from a single punch, which folds (vertical and horizontal); "
"and a square hole in the centre using folds and one straight cut across the corner that meets the centre of the sheet. For the last card two different pairs of folds work. "
"A vertical and a horizontal fold give a square tilted like a diamond, and two diagonal folds give a square with its sides parallel to the sheet, so the student makes both. "
"A wrong fold shows where the punched holes would land and how they differ from the target, and a solved card shows the sheet folded, punched and opened in three small pictures.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: how many layers does the folded sheet have? Then: each layer gets one hole, so the holes are mirror partners across each fold. Then: the fold line sits halfway between a pair of matching holes. "
"Last, the fold lines are shown dashed, and a similar card follows. Through doing, the student learns that every fold doubles the holes or cuts, and that the folds of a paper cut-out become the lines of symmetry of the opened shape."
),
"M2.S10.T10.C41.2D1": (
"The student sees a figure drawn in colour on top of a faint fixed outline of itself, with a red dot marking the centre of rotation. Below it is a turn dial from 0 to 360 degrees in steps of 15 degrees, "
"a Turn button and a record of the angles that matched.\n\n"
"The student first predicts the smallest turn that will make the figure look the same. Then the student sets the dial, presses Turn, and the figure rotates about the red dot. "
"If the figure fits exactly on its outline, the outline flashes green and the angle can be added to the record. A turn that nearly fits shows the gap in red, so a near miss cannot be recorded. "
"The student keeps turning, noting each angle that matches, and finally lists the angles in order with 360 last. A list that leaves out 360 is reminded that a full turn always matches.\n\n"
"The six figures are a 4-bladed windmill (90, 180, 270 and 360 degrees), a strip with one short side (360 degrees only, because at 180 degrees the short side lands on the wrong end), "
"a plus shape with 4 equal arms (90, 180, 270 and 360), a long rectangle that is not a square (180 and 360), a figure with 3 equal arms 120 degrees apart (120, 240 and 360), and an isosceles triangle that is not equilateral (360 only). "
"The windmill's solved card points out that no fold makes it overlap, yet it matches by turning. A solved card shows the matching positions in colour around the centre.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: where is the centre of rotation marked? Then: turn the figure slowly and watch for the outline to match. Then: note each angle where it matches, and add 360 at the end. "
"Last, the matching angles are shown in colour, and a similar figure follows. Through doing, the student learns that a figure has rotational symmetry when some turn below 360 degrees matches it, that 360 degrees always matches, "
"and that a figure whose only match is 360 degrees has no rotational symmetry."
),
"M2.S12.T12.C54.2D1": (
"At the middle of the screen is a small centre point with identical arms reaching out from it, up to 8 of them. The student has a number-of-arms stepper, a slider for each angle between neighbouring arms, "
"a Turn button and a list of angles of symmetry. A faint copy of the figure sits exactly over the fixed figure and is the part that turns.\n\n"
"The student predicts the angle of symmetry, sets the number of arms and the angles between them, then presses Turn. The copy rotates over the fixed figure and every turn at which the arms land exactly on arms is added to the list, "
"with 360 last. The gaps always add up to a full turn of 360 degrees, so changing one gap changes the last one. If the angles are unequal, the copy fails to land on the figure and the gap is shown in red. "
"The student can nudge one gap of an equal figure and watch the list shrink to just 360. The number of angles in the list, counting 360, is shown as the order of the figure.\n\n"
"The six figures are: 4 arms at 90 degrees (90, 180, 270 and 360); 4 arms where opposite arms point in opposite directions but the two pairs are not at right angles, for example gaps of 60, 120, 60 and 120 degrees (180 and 360 only); "
"3 arms at 120 degrees (120, 240 and 360); 3 arms with gaps of 100, 120 and 140 degrees (360 only); 5 arms at 72 degrees (72, 144, 216, 288 and 360); and 6 arms at 60 degrees (order 6). "
"A wrong order shows the angles counted one by one along a clock face, and a solved card shows the angle between arms written as 360 divided by the number of arms.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: how many arms does the figure have, and are they all the same? Then: for equal arms, share the full turn of 360 degrees equally among the gaps. Then: divide 360 by the number of arms to get the gap and the smallest angle. "
"Last, the angles are shown on a clock face, and a similar figure follows. Through doing, the student learns that equal arms at equal gaps of 360 divided by n give n angles of symmetry, "
"that the order is the number of angles counting 360, and that unequal spacing can leave only 360."
),
"M2.S14.T14.C58.2D1": (
"A round disc divided into 12 equal sectors sits on the left like a clock face, and a tall ladder marked from 0 to 360 degrees stands on the right. The student has a repeat-every stepper, "
"a dial that tests a target angle and a read-out for the smallest angle.\n\n"
"In the first two cards the student colours the disc so that it has a given number of angles of symmetry. The student chooses how often the colour pattern repeats around the circle, colours that block of sectors, "
"and the tool copies it all the way round. If the repeat divides 12, the copies close up and the disc matches itself after one repeat. If it does not, for example every 5 sectors, the pattern fails to close up and the leftover sectors show in red. "
"A pattern that repeats every 4 sectors gives 3 angles of symmetry, and one that repeats every 3 sectors gives 4. Trying other repeats shows that only 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 12 sectors close up.\n\n"
"In the other cards the ladder takes over. The student predicts the smallest angle before moving the stepper for the number of equal turns, and the rungs climb by that angle up to 360. "
"With a smallest angle of 60 the other angles are 120, 180, 240, 300 and 360. When 60 is an angle and two angles lie below it, the student tests smaller angles on the dial and finds the smallest is 20, with rungs 20, 40 and 60. "
"A smallest angle of 45 reaches 360 after 8 turns, so it is possible. For 17 the rungs reach 357 and then jump past 360, leaving a remainder of 3, and the read-out gives 360 divided by 17 as 21 and 3/17, which is not a whole number of turns, so 17 is not possible. "
"Setting 7 equal turns gives 51 and 3/7 degrees in the same read-out.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: how many equal turns make one full turn? Then: the smallest angle is 360 divided by the number of turns. Then: every other angle is that angle added again and again, up to 360. "
"Last, the ladder is shown rung by rung, and a similar card follows. Through doing, the student learns that the angles of symmetry are the multiples of the smallest angle, that 360 is always the top rung, "
"and that a whole-number smallest angle must be a factor of 360."
),
"M3.S18.T18.C74.2D1": (
"A tray at the bottom holds the figures, and beside it is a two-way table with rows named has a line and has no line, and columns named has a turn and has no turn. "
"Each figure has two buttons, a Fold test and a Turn test, and two counters, one for lines of symmetry and one for angles of symmetry. Here a turn means some angle below 360 degrees that matches.\n\n"
"The student picks a figure and predicts the box it belongs in. The Fold test lets the student set fold lines and press Fold, and every line that overlaps is drawn dashed on the figure and counted. "
"The Turn test shows a red centre dot and a dial, and every angle that matches is marked and counted. The two tests are separate, and the student runs both before deciding. The student then drops the figure into a box and types its two counts. "
"A figure dropped in the wrong box has both test results replayed beside it. A count of angles that ignores 360 is reminded that a full turn is always an angle of symmetry.\n\n"
"The six figures are an equilateral triangle (3 lines, 3 angles: 120, 240 and 360), a square (4 lines, 4 angles), a kite that is not a rhombus (1 line, and only 360, so 1 angle), "
"a twisted six-petal flower (0 lines, 6 angles at steps of 60), a regular hexagon (6 lines, 6 angles) and a scalene triangle (0 lines, only 360). "
"A solved card shows the fold lines and the matching turns on the figure.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: which test finds lines, and which finds turns? Then: run the fold test first, then turn the figure about its centre. Then: count the matching folds and the matching turns, including 360. "
"Last, both results are shown on the figure, the box is filled and a similar figure follows. Through doing, the student learns that lines and turns are two separate tests whose answers can differ, "
"that a figure can have lines only, turns only, both or neither, and that a regular figure with n sides has n lines and n angles of symmetry."
),
"M3.S20.T20.C83.2D1": (
"The student gets a 4 by 4 board and a tray of 16 square tiles. Each tile is cut along one diagonal into a blue triangle and a yellow triangle, so it can be turned into four different looks. "
"Above the board are a Check Lines button, a Check Turns button and a counter for the lines and angles of symmetry found.\n\n"
"The student predicts the number of lines, then places tiles in the cells and turns each one a quarter turn at a time. A tile and its mirror partner across a vertical line must slope the opposite way, with the blue and yellow triangles mirrored too. "
"Check Lines tests the vertical line, the horizontal line and both diagonals, and draws each line of symmetry it finds as a dashed line. If a line is missing, the tile that has no partner is highlighted. "
"If the design has an extra line, such as a diagonal, the line is drawn on the design so the student sees there are too many. Check Turns tests the turns and lists the angles found. For the half turn, each tile must match the tile opposite it after a turn of 180 degrees, "
"as the tile in the top left corner must match the one in the bottom right.\n\n"
"The six tasks are: make a design with exactly 1 line of symmetry; exactly 2 lines; 4 lines; a half turn but no line; complete a given quarter of the board into a design with exactly 2 lines and no diagonal line; "
"and make a design with no line and no turn, so that only 360 degrees works. A solved card shows the lines and the turns found.\n\n"
"Hints come in order. First: what must each tile match for a line to work? Then: fill one part, then copy it across the line you want. Then: check for extra lines, especially along the diagonals. "
"Last, the tile with no partner is highlighted: fix it and check again. Through doing, the student learns that a line of symmetry needs every tile to match its mirror partner, that extra lines must be checked before counting, "
"and that a design can be symmetric by a turn even when it has no line."
),
}
